KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ three levels: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ two levels: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [temp.spec.partial.match]/1, [class.static.data]/4: F<W<W<W<int>>>>
matches the partial specialization F<W<T>>, whose constant member is
F<T>::value + 1, so its value is 3.  When the instances are not
instantiated innermost first, the front end leaves the inner instances'
initializers unresolved (F<W<W<int>>>::value is `<<expr:cpp_name>> + 1' in
the symbol table) and the members are nondeterministic: both assertions
fail, silently.  Instantiating F<W<W<int>>> first makes it work; two levels
work.  test_enumerator.desc (an enumerator) and test_base_class.desc (the
value inherited from a base class) are conversion errors ("instantiating
'F' with <struct nil>").  The base-class form is libc++'s __find_max_align,
the default argument of std::aligned_storage: `aligned_storage<24>::type'
fails, so std::function's __value_func::__buf_ is missing and libc++'s
~__value_func (function.h:394) is dropped (cpp11_function_basic_libcxx).
Pre-existing (bin53).  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept these programs, and they
fail only their WRONG assertion.
